  ('abcb8c6c-b827-554e-8f39-13b0ca8653ab', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g6-precise-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Read the sentences: "The volunteers cleaned the beach. They picked up bottles and wrappers. They filled twelve bags." Which revision combines them correctly into one varied sentence?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Picking up bottles and wrappers, the volunteers cleaned the beach and filled twelve bags.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The volunteers cleaned the beach, and they picked up bottles and wrappers, and they filled twelve bags.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"and-chain"},{"text":"Picking up bottles and wrappers, twelve bags were filled by the volunteers cleaning the beach.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dangling-modifier"},{"text":"The volunteers cleaned the beach, they picked up bottles and wrappers, they filled twelve bags.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Picking up bottles and wrappers, the volunteers cleaned the beach and filled twelve bags.$q$, $q$The opening phrase describes the volunteers, and "the volunteers" comes right after it, so the sentence is clear. In "Picking up bottles and wrappers, twelve bags were filled," the phrase lands next to "twelve bags," which makes it sound like the bags picked up the trash.$q$, 'draft', null)
