  ('cf334a83-504a-5f27-b0ee-c955722879b1', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g6-precise-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Read the paragraph: "The comet appeared in March. It was visible without a telescope. People gathered on hills to watch it. It faded by April." Which revision best varies sentence length and structure without changing the meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"In March, the comet appeared. In March, it was visible without a telescope. In April, it faded. People gathered on hills.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"repeats-opening-pattern"},{"text":"Visible without a telescope, the comet appeared in March. People gathered on hills to watch it, but by April it had faded.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Appearing in March and visible without a telescope and watched from hills, the comet faded by April.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overloaded-sentence"},{"text":"The comet appeared in March, it was visible without a telescope, people gathered on hills, it faded by April.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-chain"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Visible without a telescope, the comet appeared in March. People gathered on hills to watch it, but by April it had faded.$q$, $q$This revision opens one sentence with a describing phrase and joins two ideas with "but," keeping every fact from the original. The version that starts three sentences with "In March" or "In April" repeats the same opening pattern and moves the hill watching to the end, which confuses the order.$q$, 'draft', null)
